"comparison":"Both GS and BAC utilize stress testing as a core component of their capital adequacy and risk assessment processes. GS explicitly states that firmwide stress tests are performed on a comprehensive range of financial and nonfinancial risks to 'assess and mitigate our risk positions' and 'assess capital adequacy' (gs_2024_7_P298_S002, gs_2024_7_P298_S005). Similarly, BAC conducts an Internal Capital Adequacy Assessment Process (ICAAP) which incorporates 'periodic stress tests' to assess potential impacts on regulatory capital under baseline and adverse conditions (bac_2024_7_P181_S002, bac_2024_7_P181_S003). While GS emphasizes 'tail risks' and 'idiosyncratic risks' in its firmwide scope, BAC frames the stress tests within the ICAAP framework alongside qualitative risk assessments to identify material risks not fully captured in forecasts.",
"target_sentence_ids":["gs_2024_7_P298_S002","gs_2024_7_P298_S003","gs_2024_7_P298_S005"],
"peer_sentence_ids":["bac_2024_7_P181_S002","bac_2024_7_P181_S003","bac_2024_7_P181_S004"]
},
