"peer_ticker":"C",
"peer_disclosure_id":"c_2024_7_D272",
"relationship":"related_topic",
"comparison":"Both GS and Citi perform stress testing to quantify exposure to adverse events, but they focus on different risk dimensions. GS performs 'firmwide stress tests' covering 'financial and nonfinancial risks' including 'systemic and emerging risks' to ensure a 'comprehensive analysis of our vulnerabilities' (gs_2024_7_P298_S003). Citi, in contrast, highlights 'liquidity stress testing' performed for 'each of Citi's major entities, operating subsidiaries and countries' to 'quantify the potential impact of an adverse liquidity event on the balance sheet and liquidity position' (c_2024_7_P399_S001, c_2024_7_P399_S002). While both aim to ensure sufficient resources (GS for capital adequacy, Citi for liquidity), Citi's disclosure specifies the scope as liquidity mismatches over various time horizons (c_2024_7_P400_S001), whereas GS emphasizes the breadth of risk categories including operational and compliance risks.",
"target_sentence_ids":["gs_2024_7_P298_S003","gs_2024_7_P298_S005"],
"peer_sentence_ids":["c_2024_7_P399_S001","c_2024_7_P399_S002","c_2024_7_P400_S001"]
},
{
"peer_ticker":"JPM",
"peer_disclosure_id":"jpm_2024_7_D275",
"relationship":"related_topic",
"comparison":"Both GS and JPM emphasize the regular and event-driven nature of their stress testing. GS performs 'firmwide stress tests' on a 'regular basis' and also 'ad hoc stress tests in anticipation of market events or conditions' (gs_2024_7_P298_S004). JPM conducts 'internal liquidity stress testing' on a 'daily basis', with other stress tests performed 'in response to specific market events or concerns' (jpm_2024_7_P402_S001, jpm_2024_7_P402_S002). A key difference is the frequency and scope of the 'internal' vs 'firmwide' label: JPM specifies daily internal liquidity stress testing, while GS describes firmwide tests covering a broader array of risks (credit, market, operational, etc.) rather than just liquidity, though both use stress tests to manage capital and risk positions.",
"target_sentence_ids":["gs_2024_7_P298_S001","gs_2024_7_P298_S004"],
"peer_sentence_ids":["jpm_2024_7_P402_S001","jpm_2024_7_P402_S002"]
},
{
"peer_ticker":"MS",
"peer_disclosure_id":"ms_2024_7_D128",
"relationship":"related_topic",
"comparison":"Both GS and MS incorporate 'idiosyncratic' risks into their stress testing scenarios. GS describes its firmwide stress tests as combining risks to ensure analysis of 'vulnerabilities and idiosyncratic risks' (gs_2024_7_P298_S003). MS uses Liquidity Stress Tests to model flows across scenarios containing 'various combinations of idiosyncratic and systemic stress events of different severity and duration' (ms_2024_7_P155_S002). Both firms integrate these specific risk factors into their broader risk management frameworks, with GS focusing on the firmwide capital adequacy assessment and MS focusing on the Required Liquidity Framework, but both explicitly acknowledge the dual nature of stress events (idiosyncratic vs systemic) in their methodology.",
